1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,460
In this video, we'll learn
the basics of weather the easy way,

2
00:00:04,460 --> 00:00:08,419
and see how meteorologists use
them to read the weather and

3
00:00:08,419 --> 00:00:10,700
follow a real storm over one town.

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,450
What is weather made of?

5
00:00:13,800 --> 00:00:17,926
Let's see what air pressure,
temperature, humidity and wind are,

6
00:00:17,926 --> 00:00:22,825
why they behave the way they do, and
how they come together in a real storm.

7
00:00:23,625 --> 00:00:26,050
Let's start with the air itself.

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:30,787
The atmosphere is the layer of air
that wraps around the Earth,

9
00:00:30,787 --> 00:00:32,250
held down by gravity.

10
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:35,675
It's much thinner than
most pictures suggest.

11
00:00:36,025 --> 00:00:40,684
The layer where our weather happens
is only about twelve kilometres deep,

12
00:00:40,684 --> 00:00:42,279
while the Earth's radius,

13
00:00:42,279 --> 00:00:45,087
the distance from its centre
to its surface,

14
00:00:45,087 --> 00:00:48,725
is six thousand three hundred
and seventy-one kilometres.

15
00:00:49,075 --> 00:00:51,175
How thin is that, really?

16
00:00:51,525 --> 00:00:56,755
Let's shrink the whole Earth down to a
desk globe, thirty centimetres across,

17
00:00:56,755 --> 00:01:01,850
so its radius is fifteen centimetres,
or one hundred and fifty millimetres.

18
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:06,920
First, we find what fraction of the
Earth's radius the weather layer is:

19
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:10,066
twelve divided by six
thousand three hundred and

20
00:01:10,066 --> 00:01:12,950
seventy-one, which
is about two thousandths.

21
00:01:13,300 --> 00:01:16,926
Then we shrink the layer by the same
factor as the Earth:

22
00:01:16,926 --> 00:01:19,088
two thousandths of one hundred and

23
00:01:19,088 --> 00:01:22,650
fifty millimetres is about
three tenths of a millimetre.

24
00:01:24,150 --> 00:01:28,475
That's like three sheets of paper,
painted onto the desk globe.

25
00:01:28,825 --> 00:01:33,820
So from now on, we'll draw the air
fifty times thicker than it really is,

26
00:01:33,820 --> 00:01:35,325
just so we can see it.

27
00:01:36,125 --> 00:01:38,475
Air is a mixture of gases.

28
00:01:38,825 --> 00:01:42,763
By volume, about seventy-eight
percent is nitrogen,

29
00:01:42,763 --> 00:01:47,550
twenty-one percent is oxygen, and almost
one percent is argon.

30
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:52,088
Carbon dioxide is only about
four hundredths of one percent,

31
00:01:52,088 --> 00:01:56,975
and water vapour comes and goes, from
almost nothing to a few percent.

32
00:01:57,325 --> 00:02:02,466
All of it is made of molecules that zip
around at hundreds of metres per second,

33
00:02:02,466 --> 00:02:06,000
bumping into each other
billions of times every second.

34
00:02:06,350 --> 00:02:11,875
We can't follow single molecules,
so meteorologists talk about air parcels.

35
00:02:12,225 --> 00:02:16,499
An air parcel is a small volume
of air that moves with the flow:

36
00:02:16,499 --> 00:02:19,103
big enough to hold countless molecules,

37
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:22,175
but small compared
with the weather around it.

38
00:02:22,525 --> 00:02:25,393
Here's one, drawn far bigger than life,

39
00:02:25,393 --> 00:02:28,850
riding the real
wind over north-western France.

40
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:31,936
Wherever the wind goes, the parcel goes,

41
00:02:31,936 --> 00:02:36,450
and it carries its own temperature
and water vapour along with it.

42
00:02:37,250 --> 00:02:39,600
Now let's look at air pressure.

43
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:44,005
Those fast molecules keep
hitting every surface they meet:

44
00:02:44,005 --> 00:02:47,850
your skin, the walls
of a room, the surface of the sea.

45
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:53,150
Each hit is a tiny push, and billions
of them add up to a steady force.

46
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:57,650
Pressure is that force
divided by the area it pushes on.

47
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,875
In air at rest, it pushes equally
in every direction: up, down and sideways.

48
00:03:04,225 --> 00:03:08,975
Its unit is the pascal, one newton
of force on each square metre.

49
00:03:09,775 --> 00:03:14,075
So how big is the pressure at the
ground, and where does it come from?

50
00:03:14,425 --> 00:03:18,747
Picture a tall column of air standing
on one square metre of ground,

51
00:03:18,747 --> 00:03:21,925
reaching all the way
to the top of the atmosphere.

52
00:03:22,275 --> 00:03:27,375
Gravity pulls all that air down, and
the ground has to hold up its weight.

53
00:03:27,725 --> 00:03:31,356
At sea level, the average
pressure is one hundred and

54
00:03:31,356 --> 00:03:34,850
one thousand three hundred
and twenty-five pascals.

55
00:03:35,200 --> 00:03:39,177
Weight is mass times g, where g is
the pull of gravity,

56
00:03:39,177 --> 00:03:42,575
nine point eight one
metres per second squared.

57
00:03:42,925 --> 00:03:47,200
So the mass in our column
is the pressure divided by g.

58
00:03:47,550 --> 00:03:53,056
That's about ten thousand three hundred
kilograms of air above every square metre,

59
00:03:53,056 --> 00:03:54,600
as heavy as seven cars.

60
00:03:56,100 --> 00:04:00,333
So the pressure at the ground
is the weight of all the air above it,

61
00:04:00,333 --> 00:04:02,200
spread over each square metre.

62
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:05,050
Why doesn't this crush us?

63
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:09,728
Because the air pushes from every
side at once, and the air and

64
00:04:09,728 --> 00:04:13,300
fluids inside our bodies
push back out just as hard.

65
00:04:13,650 --> 00:04:16,675
The pushes balance, so we don't feel them.

66
00:04:17,025 --> 00:04:20,450
We only notice when the balance
breaks for a moment:

67
00:04:20,450 --> 00:04:23,678
your ears pop in a fast
lift or a climbing plane,

68
00:04:23,678 --> 00:04:28,025
because the outside pressure changes
before the inside catches up.

69
00:04:28,825 --> 00:04:33,575
Pascals give big numbers,
so weather maps use hectopascals.

70
00:04:33,925 --> 00:04:36,825
One hectopascal is one hundred pascals,

71
00:04:36,825 --> 00:04:42,700
so the average pressure at sea level
is one thousand and thirteen hectopascals.

72
00:04:43,050 --> 00:04:47,299
Older maps use millibars,
which are exactly the same size,

73
00:04:47,299 --> 00:04:49,350
so the numbers don't change.

74
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:53,775
The instrument that measures
pressure is called a barometer.

75
00:04:54,125 --> 00:04:59,450
Day to day, the reading at sea level
moves up or down by only a few percent.

76
00:04:59,800 --> 00:05:04,100
Yet those few percent are what
drive the winds, as we'll see.

77
00:05:04,900 --> 00:05:06,750
Next, density.

78
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:11,300
Density tells us how much mass
is packed into each cubic metre,

79
00:05:11,300 --> 00:05:14,100
and we write it with the Greek letter rho.

80
00:05:14,450 --> 00:05:19,975
Air near the ground has a density of about
one point two kilograms per cubic metre.

81
00:05:20,325 --> 00:05:24,287
So a room five metres long, four
metres wide and two and

82
00:05:24,287 --> 00:05:27,896
a half metres high holds
fifty cubic metres of air,

83
00:05:27,896 --> 00:05:31,575
which is about sixty kilograms:
as much as a person.

84
00:05:33,075 --> 00:05:37,439
Temperature measures how fast
the molecules jiggle on average:

85
00:05:37,439 --> 00:05:40,325
the warmer the air, the faster they move.

86
00:05:40,675 --> 00:05:45,500
Weather reports use degrees Celsius,
but physics uses kelvin.

87
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:48,216
To get kelvin, add two hundred and

88
00:05:48,216 --> 00:05:51,764
seventy-three point one five
to the Celsius number,

89
00:05:51,764 --> 00:05:57,400
so fifteen degrees Celsius is two hundred
and eighty-eight point one five kelvin.

90
00:05:57,750 --> 00:06:01,950
A kelvin step is the same
size as a degree Celsius step;

91
00:06:01,950 --> 00:06:04,200
only the starting point moves.

92
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:07,714
Zero kelvin is the coldest
anything can be,

93
00:06:07,714 --> 00:06:11,025
so a temperature in kelvin
is never negative.

94
00:06:11,375 --> 00:06:15,550
That's why the gas law, which
comes next, uses kelvin.

95
00:06:16,350 --> 00:06:20,175
Now let's connect pressure,
density and temperature.

96
00:06:20,525 --> 00:06:24,325
Think about what makes
the molecules push harder on a wall.

97
00:06:24,675 --> 00:06:27,731
If we pack more molecules
into each cubic metre,

98
00:06:27,731 --> 00:06:32,825
more of them hit the wall each second, so
the pressure goes up with the density.

99
00:06:33,175 --> 00:06:36,702
If the molecules move
faster, each one hits harder and

100
00:06:36,702 --> 00:06:40,425
more often, so the pressure
goes up with the temperature.

101
00:06:40,775 --> 00:06:46,195
Put together, the pressure is proportional
to density times temperature:

102
00:06:46,195 --> 00:06:47,475
p equals rho R T.

103
00:06:47,825 --> 00:06:50,525
This is called the ideal gas law.

104
00:06:50,875 --> 00:06:53,293
Here p is the pressure in pascals,

105
00:06:53,293 --> 00:06:56,920
rho is the density,
T is the temperature in kelvin,

106
00:06:56,920 --> 00:07:01,258
and R is a fixed number for dry
air, called the gas constant:

107
00:07:01,258 --> 00:07:05,525
two hundred and eighty-seven
joules per kilogram per kelvin.

108
00:07:06,325 --> 00:07:08,025
Let's use it.

109
00:07:08,375 --> 00:07:11,257
What is the density of air at sea level,

110
00:07:11,257 --> 00:07:15,725
at one thousand and thirteen
hectopascals and fifteen degrees?

111
00:07:16,075 --> 00:07:20,675
We turn the law around:
rho equals p divided by R T.

112
00:07:21,025 --> 00:07:25,792
That's one hundred and one thousand
three hundred and twenty-five pascals,

113
00:07:25,792 --> 00:07:29,721
divided by two hundred
and eighty-seven times two hundred and

114
00:07:29,721 --> 00:07:31,525
eighty-eight point one five.

115
00:07:31,875 --> 00:07:36,452
The answer is about one point two two five
kilograms per cubic metre,

116
00:07:36,452 --> 00:07:38,575
the number we used for the room.

117
00:07:40,075 --> 00:07:44,500
Now let's keep the pressure fixed
and change only the temperature.

118
00:07:44,850 --> 00:07:45,972
At zero degrees,

119
00:07:45,972 --> 00:07:51,300
the density comes out at about one point
two nine kilograms per cubic metre.

120
00:07:51,650 --> 00:07:55,350
At thirty degrees,
it's about one point one six.

121
00:07:55,700 --> 00:08:01,075
So at the same pressure, warm air is about
ten percent lighter than cold air.

122
00:08:02,575 --> 00:08:05,097
That's why a hot-air balloon floats,

123
00:08:05,097 --> 00:08:09,300
and why warm air tends to rise
while cold air tends to sink.

124
00:08:09,650 --> 00:08:12,400
We'll meet this again with weather fronts.

125
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:17,250
Now let's work out how the pressure
changes as we go up.

126
00:08:17,600 --> 00:08:21,175
Our goal is a rule for how fast
it falls with height.

127
00:08:21,525 --> 00:08:24,564
Take a thin slab of air inside our column,

128
00:08:24,564 --> 00:08:29,050
standing on one square
metre, with a small thickness, delta z.

129
00:08:29,400 --> 00:08:33,700
Here z is height, and
delta z means a small step in height.

130
00:08:34,050 --> 00:08:38,000
The slab is at rest, so
the forces on it must balance.

131
00:08:38,350 --> 00:08:43,483
The air below pushes it up, with a force
equal to the pressure at its bottom,

132
00:08:43,483 --> 00:08:45,950
because the area is one square metre.

133
00:08:46,300 --> 00:08:50,250
The air above pushes it
down, with the pressure at its top.

134
00:08:50,600 --> 00:08:53,375
And gravity pulls it down with its weight.

135
00:08:54,175 --> 00:08:56,225
What does the slab weigh?

136
00:08:56,575 --> 00:09:02,491
First, its volume: its base is one square
metre and its thickness is delta z,

137
00:09:02,491 --> 00:09:07,100
so its volume, capital V, is one
square metre times delta z.

138
00:09:07,450 --> 00:09:13,030
Next, its mass: mass is density times
volume, so m equals rho times V,

139
00:09:13,030 --> 00:09:17,175
which is rho, times one
square metre, times delta z.

140
00:09:17,525 --> 00:09:21,051
Last, its weight: weight is mass times g,

141
00:09:21,051 --> 00:09:25,350
so W equals rho g delta z, times one
square metre.

142
00:09:26,850 --> 00:09:28,550
Now the balance.

143
00:09:28,900 --> 00:09:34,100
Each push is a pressure times the area
it pushes on, one square metre.

144
00:09:34,450 --> 00:09:38,125
The push up, minus the push
down, equals the weight.

145
00:09:38,475 --> 00:09:42,962
The square metre appears in every
term, so we can cancel it:

146
00:09:42,962 --> 00:09:47,300
p at the bottom, minus p at
the top, equals rho g delta z.

147
00:09:47,650 --> 00:09:49,962
The pressure at the bottom is bigger,

148
00:09:49,962 --> 00:09:53,400
by exactly the weight
of the slab on each square metre.

149
00:09:54,200 --> 00:09:56,075
Let's tidy this up.

150
00:09:56,425 --> 00:09:59,834
The pressure at the top
minus the pressure at the

151
00:09:59,834 --> 00:10:03,243
bottom is the change
in pressure across the slab,

152
00:10:03,243 --> 00:10:08,125
delta p. It's negative, because
the pressure falls as we go up.

153
00:10:08,475 --> 00:10:12,300
So delta p equals minus rho g delta z.

154
00:10:12,650 --> 00:10:17,825
Divide both sides by delta z, and let
the slab get thinner and thinner.

155
00:10:18,175 --> 00:10:21,615
Delta p over delta z becomes
the derivative,

156
00:10:21,615 --> 00:10:26,775
partial p by partial z: how fast
the pressure changes with height.

157
00:10:27,125 --> 00:10:32,000
Partial means only the height changes;
the place and the time stay fixed.

158
00:10:32,350 --> 00:10:36,575
So partial p by partial z equals
minus rho g.

159
00:10:36,925 --> 00:10:40,379
This is called hydrostatic
balance: at every height,

160
00:10:40,379 --> 00:10:43,700
the pressure holds up
the weight of the air above.

161
00:10:44,500 --> 00:10:47,050
How fast is that near the ground?

162
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:52,182
Rho is about one point two two
five, and g is nine point eight one,

163
00:10:52,182 --> 00:10:57,250
so the pressure falls by about twelve
pascals for every metre we climb.

164
00:10:58,750 --> 00:11:02,800
That's one hectopascal
for about every eight metres.

165
00:11:03,150 --> 00:11:06,905
Ride a lift to the top
of a three-hundred-metre tower,

166
00:11:06,905 --> 00:11:10,800
and the pressure drops by about
thirty-six hectopascals.

167
00:11:12,300 --> 00:11:14,800
That's the change your ears notice.

168
00:11:15,150 --> 00:11:19,497
Hydrostatic balance holds very
well for large weather systems,

169
00:11:19,497 --> 00:11:24,475
because their up and down accelerations
are tiny compared with gravity.

170
00:11:25,275 --> 00:11:29,375
But rho isn't fixed: the higher
we go, the thinner the air.

171
00:11:29,725 --> 00:11:32,050
So let's bring in the gas law.

172
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:35,425
It says rho equals p over R T.

173
00:11:35,775 --> 00:11:40,050
Let's put that into hydrostatic
balance, in place of rho.

174
00:11:40,400 --> 00:11:45,850
We get partial p by partial z equals
minus p g over R T.

175
00:11:46,200 --> 00:11:51,300
Now the fall in pressure per metre
is proportional to the pressure itself.

176
00:11:51,650 --> 00:11:55,500
Where the pressure is high, near
the ground, it falls fast.

177
00:11:55,850 --> 00:11:59,800
Higher up, where there's less
pressure, it falls more slowly.

178
00:12:00,600 --> 00:12:03,893
Look at the new rule: the three numbers R,

179
00:12:03,893 --> 00:12:07,500
T and g always appear
together, as g over R T.

180
00:12:07,850 --> 00:12:11,370
So let's give the upside-down
group, R T over g,

181
00:12:11,370 --> 00:12:14,450
its own name: the scale height, capital H.

182
00:12:14,800 --> 00:12:17,922
With it, our rule becomes much simpler:

183
00:12:17,922 --> 00:12:21,525
partial p by partial z equals
minus p over H.

184
00:12:21,875 --> 00:12:24,175
What kind of quantity is H?

185
00:12:24,525 --> 00:12:26,575
Let's check its units.

186
00:12:26,925 --> 00:12:29,601
R is in joules per kilogram per kelvin,

187
00:12:29,601 --> 00:12:33,925
and a joule per kilogram is a metre
squared per second squared.

188
00:12:34,275 --> 00:12:39,353
Multiply by T in kelvin, divide
by g in metres per second squared,

189
00:12:39,353 --> 00:12:42,200
and we're left with metres: a height.

190
00:12:42,550 --> 00:12:44,725
So what does this height mean?

191
00:12:45,075 --> 00:12:50,130
The rule says that if the pressure kept
falling at the rate it has at the start,

192
00:12:50,130 --> 00:12:53,100
it would reach zero
after a climb of exactly H.

193
00:12:53,450 --> 00:12:55,405
It never actually gets there,

194
00:12:55,405 --> 00:12:59,248
because the fall slows down
as the pressure gets smaller,

195
00:12:59,248 --> 00:13:02,350
but H tells us the size
of climb that matters.

196
00:13:02,700 --> 00:13:08,087
So H is one number that tells us how deep
the atmosphere is: the bigger H is,

197
00:13:08,087 --> 00:13:12,775
the more slowly the pressure
falls, and the higher the air reaches.

198
00:13:13,125 --> 00:13:15,795
And because T is on top of the fraction,

199
00:13:15,795 --> 00:13:20,600
warmer air has a bigger scale height
and reaches higher than colder air.

200
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:23,450
So how deep is our air?

201
00:13:23,800 --> 00:13:27,350
Let's work out H for a typical
day at sea level.

202
00:13:27,700 --> 00:13:32,500
R is two hundred and eighty-seven,
the gas constant for dry air.

203
00:13:32,850 --> 00:13:38,500
T is fifteen degrees, which is two hundred
and eighty-eight point one five kelvin.

204
00:13:38,850 --> 00:13:43,000
And g is nine point eight one
metres per second squared.

205
00:13:43,350 --> 00:13:47,277
So H is two hundred
and eighty-seven times two hundred and

206
00:13:47,277 --> 00:13:49,173
eighty-eight point one five,

207
00:13:49,173 --> 00:13:54,725
divided by nine point eight one, which is
about eight point four three kilometres.

208
00:13:56,225 --> 00:13:59,250
Here's a nice way to picture that number.

209
00:13:59,600 --> 00:14:05,029
Imagine squashing all the air in our
column to the same density all the way up:

210
00:14:05,029 --> 00:14:06,540
the sea-level density,

211
00:14:06,540 --> 00:14:12,175
one point two two five kilograms per cubic
metre, which we found with the gas law.

212
00:14:12,525 --> 00:14:17,700
How tall would that column be, if it still
had to weigh the same as the real one?

213
00:14:18,050 --> 00:14:22,321
Its weight on each square metre
would be rho g times its height,

214
00:14:22,321 --> 00:14:25,725
and that must equal
the sea-level pressure, p zero.

215
00:14:26,075 --> 00:14:31,857
So its height is p zero over rho g: one
hundred and one thousand three hundred and

216
00:14:31,857 --> 00:14:37,075
twenty-five, divided by one point two
two five times nine point eight one.

217
00:14:37,425 --> 00:14:41,025
That's again about
eight point four three kilometres.

218
00:14:42,525 --> 00:14:45,885
So the scale height is how tall
the atmosphere would

219
00:14:45,885 --> 00:14:49,375
be if all its air were
as dense as it is at sea level.

220
00:14:50,175 --> 00:14:54,325
Now let's solve this rule, to find
the pressure at any height.

221
00:14:54,675 --> 00:15:00,202
To keep the steps simple, let's assume the
temperature is the same at every height,

222
00:15:00,202 --> 00:15:02,200
so that H is one fixed number.

223
00:15:02,550 --> 00:15:06,883
That's a simplification: the real
temperature changes with height,

224
00:15:06,883 --> 00:15:12,200
as we'll see soon, but it gives us a clean
formula that makes a good first guess.

225
00:15:12,550 --> 00:15:16,312
First, divide both sides by p: one over p,

226
00:15:16,312 --> 00:15:21,150
times partial p by partial z, equals
minus one over H.

227
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:24,041
Here's a useful fact from calculus:

228
00:15:24,041 --> 00:15:27,525
the slope of the natural
log of p is one over p.

229
00:15:27,875 --> 00:15:29,347
So, by the chain rule,

230
00:15:29,347 --> 00:15:34,500
the left side is just the rate of change
of the natural log of p with height.

231
00:15:34,850 --> 00:15:38,696
That means the log
of p falls by the same amount,

232
00:15:38,696 --> 00:15:41,600
one over H, for every metre we climb.

233
00:15:42,400 --> 00:15:47,108
Now we add up those small falls, from
the ground, where z is zero and

234
00:15:47,108 --> 00:15:51,475
the pressure is p zero, up
to height z, where the pressure is p.

235
00:15:51,825 --> 00:15:57,525
Adding up small changes is what an
integral does, so we integrate both sides.

236
00:15:57,875 --> 00:16:03,274
On the left, adding up the changes
in the log of p gives the log of p,

237
00:16:03,274 --> 00:16:05,125
minus the log of p zero.

238
00:16:05,475 --> 00:16:09,142
On the right, minus one
over H for every metre,

239
00:16:09,142 --> 00:16:13,200
over a climb of z metres, adds
up to minus z over H.

240
00:16:13,550 --> 00:16:19,450
And the log of p, minus the log
of p zero, is the log of p over p zero.

241
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:24,400
So the log of p over p zero
equals minus z over H.

242
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:28,494
The natural log is built
on a special number called e,

243
00:16:28,494 --> 00:16:30,297
about two point seven two,

244
00:16:30,297 --> 00:16:35,150
which turns up with growth and decay
just as pi turns up with circles.

245
00:16:35,500 --> 00:16:39,696
To undo the log, we
raise e to the power of each side,

246
00:16:39,696 --> 00:16:44,125
and we get p equals p zero
times e to the minus z over H.

247
00:16:45,625 --> 00:16:48,352
This tells us the pressure
at any height z,

248
00:16:48,352 --> 00:16:51,650
in air that has the same
temperature all the way up.

249
00:16:52,450 --> 00:16:54,600
What does this curve tell us?

250
00:16:54,950 --> 00:16:59,150
Let's climb exactly one
scale height, so z equals H.

251
00:16:59,500 --> 00:17:03,601
Then the power
is minus H over H, which is minus one,

252
00:17:03,601 --> 00:17:09,250
and e to the minus one is one divided
by e: about zero point three seven.

253
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:13,997
So every time we climb one scale
height, eight point four kilometres,

254
00:17:13,997 --> 00:17:18,075
the pressure falls to about
thirty-seven percent of what it was.

255
00:17:19,575 --> 00:17:21,698
From the ground's one thousand and

256
00:17:21,698 --> 00:17:25,007
thirteen hectopascals,
that's about three hundred and

257
00:17:25,007 --> 00:17:27,816
seventy-three at
eight point four kilometres,

258
00:17:27,816 --> 00:17:31,625
and about one hundred and thirty-seven
at sixteen point nine.

259
00:17:32,425 --> 00:17:36,550
Now let's zoom in on a single
kilometre near the ground.

260
00:17:36,900 --> 00:17:42,100
Our rule says that each metre we climb
takes away one over H of the pressure.

261
00:17:42,450 --> 00:17:45,561
So one kilometre takes
away about one kilometre

262
00:17:45,561 --> 00:17:48,539
divided by
eight point four three kilometres,

263
00:17:48,539 --> 00:17:50,525
which is about twelve percent.

264
00:17:50,875 --> 00:17:55,260
But the pressure shrinks a little
as we climb through that kilometre,

265
00:17:55,260 --> 00:17:57,421
so the real fall is slightly less:

266
00:17:57,421 --> 00:18:01,043
one minus e to the minus one
over eight point four three,

267
00:18:01,043 --> 00:18:02,950
which is about eleven percent.

268
00:18:04,450 --> 00:18:06,550
Eleven percent of what?

269
00:18:06,900 --> 00:18:10,325
Of the pressure at the bottom
of that kilometre.

270
00:18:10,675 --> 00:18:13,663
So the first kilometre
takes one thousand and

271
00:18:13,663 --> 00:18:16,916
thirteen hectopascals down
to about nine hundred,

272
00:18:16,916 --> 00:18:20,900
and the next takes nine hundred
down to about eight hundred.

273
00:18:21,250 --> 00:18:24,128
Each kilometre takes
away the same fraction,

274
00:18:24,128 --> 00:18:27,857
so the drops get smaller as we
go up: about a hundred and

275
00:18:27,857 --> 00:18:30,801
thirteen hectopascals
in the first kilometre,

276
00:18:30,801 --> 00:18:33,875
but only about a hundred
and one in the second.

277
00:18:34,675 --> 00:18:37,825
Here's another way to see
why the curve bends.

278
00:18:38,175 --> 00:18:43,266
Let's cut our column into ten slabs
that each hold the same amount of air:

279
00:18:43,266 --> 00:18:48,425
one tenth of the total, about a thousand
kilograms above each square metre.

280
00:18:48,775 --> 00:18:52,270
The bottom slab is only about
nine hundred metres thick,

281
00:18:52,270 --> 00:18:57,200
because it carries the weight of all
the air above it and is squeezed the most.

282
00:18:57,550 --> 00:19:00,921
Each slab higher up has less
air pressing down on it,

283
00:19:00,921 --> 00:19:03,275
so it's squeezed less and is thicker.

284
00:19:03,625 --> 00:19:07,352
The ninth slab reaches up
to about nineteen kilometres,

285
00:19:07,352 --> 00:19:10,875
and the last one thins out
with no sharp top at all.

286
00:19:11,225 --> 00:19:16,210
It's like a stack of foam mattresses:
the bottom ones are squeezed the most,

287
00:19:16,210 --> 00:19:18,900
because they carry everything above them.

288
00:19:19,250 --> 00:19:23,150
Unlike mattresses,
though, the air's layers aren't separate,

289
00:19:23,150 --> 00:19:27,050
and how much they squeeze also
depends on their temperature.

290
00:19:27,850 --> 00:19:31,400
Now let's find the height
where the pressure has halved.

291
00:19:31,750 --> 00:19:37,051
Start again from the log
of p over p zero equals minus z over H,

292
00:19:37,051 --> 00:19:39,950
and multiply both sides by minus H.

293
00:19:40,300 --> 00:19:45,953
The log of a fraction turned upside
down is minus the log of the fraction,

294
00:19:45,953 --> 00:19:49,925
so we get z equals H times
the log of p zero over p.

295
00:19:50,275 --> 00:19:54,250
This formula gives the height
for any pressure we choose.

296
00:19:54,600 --> 00:19:58,294
For half the ground pressure,
p zero over p is two,

297
00:19:58,294 --> 00:20:02,350
so z equals eight point four three
times the log of two.

298
00:20:02,700 --> 00:20:05,987
The log of two is about
zero point six nine,

299
00:20:05,987 --> 00:20:09,125
so z is about five point eight kilometres.

300
00:20:10,625 --> 00:20:14,679
So the pressure halves about
every five point eight kilometres:

301
00:20:14,679 --> 00:20:18,925
to about five hundred hectopascals
at five point eight kilometres,

302
00:20:18,925 --> 00:20:22,400
and about two hundred and fifty
at eleven point seven.

303
00:20:23,200 --> 00:20:26,150
Does the real atmosphere
follow this curve?

304
00:20:26,500 --> 00:20:29,550
Let's check it against a real
weather balloon.

305
00:20:29,900 --> 00:20:33,417
Twice a day, at hundreds
of places around the world,

306
00:20:33,417 --> 00:20:36,257
meteorologists launch a balloon carrying a

307
00:20:36,257 --> 00:20:39,300
small instrument package
called a radiosonde.

308
00:20:39,650 --> 00:20:43,726
As it rises, it radios back
the pressure, the temperature and

309
00:20:43,726 --> 00:20:47,000
the humidity,
and from its drift we get the wind.

310
00:20:47,350 --> 00:20:50,503
This one was launched near
Paris around midnight,

311
00:20:50,503 --> 00:20:54,300
at the start of the first
of November, twenty twenty-three.

312
00:20:54,650 --> 00:20:59,680
In the picture, heights are drawn ten
times taller than distances on the map,

313
00:20:59,680 --> 00:21:01,575
so that we can see the climb.

314
00:21:01,925 --> 00:21:05,950
Near the ground, its pressure
follows our curve closely.

315
00:21:06,300 --> 00:21:09,526
Higher up, the real pressure
falls a little faster,

316
00:21:09,526 --> 00:21:14,775
because the real air up there is colder,
and colder air has a smaller scale height.

317
00:21:15,125 --> 00:21:17,283
So our curve is a good first guess,

318
00:21:17,283 --> 00:21:20,675
and the measurements tell us
where it needs correcting.

319
00:21:21,475 --> 00:21:24,286
Since pressure is the weight
of the air above,

320
00:21:24,286 --> 00:21:28,625
the pressure at any height also tells
us how much air is left above it.

321
00:21:28,975 --> 00:21:33,467
At the ground, the pressure p zero
is the weight of the whole column,

322
00:21:33,467 --> 00:21:38,025
and at height z, the pressure p is
the weight of only the air above z.

323
00:21:38,375 --> 00:21:42,823
So p over p zero is the share
of all the air that's above us,

324
00:21:42,823 --> 00:21:46,250
and the share below is one
minus p over p zero.

325
00:21:46,600 --> 00:21:49,763
This time, let's use
the standard atmosphere,

326
00:21:49,763 --> 00:21:54,050
an average of real measurements
that gets colder as we go up.

327
00:21:54,400 --> 00:21:59,450
At about five and a half kilometres,
its pressure is about five hundred and

328
00:21:59,450 --> 00:22:03,625
five hectopascals, close to half
of one thousand and thirteen.

329
00:22:03,975 --> 00:22:06,644
So p over p zero is about one half,

330
00:22:06,644 --> 00:22:11,525
and one minus a half is a half:
half of all the air is below us.

331
00:22:13,025 --> 00:22:16,720
That's a little lower than our
five point eight kilometres,

332
00:22:16,720 --> 00:22:19,600
because colder air
has a smaller scale height.

333
00:22:19,950 --> 00:22:25,166
At sixteen kilometres, the pressure
is about one tenth of its ground value,

334
00:22:25,166 --> 00:22:27,600
so nine tenths of the air is below.

335
00:22:27,950 --> 00:22:33,325
And by about thirty-one kilometres,
ninety-nine percent of the air is below.

336
00:22:33,675 --> 00:22:36,925
That's why the atmosphere
is such a thin shell.

337
00:22:37,725 --> 00:22:41,925
Now let's look at the temperature
that the same balloon measured.

338
00:22:42,275 --> 00:22:45,775
Here, height goes up
and temperature goes across.

339
00:22:46,125 --> 00:22:51,347
Near the ground it's mild, and as the
balloon climbs, the temperature falls,

340
00:22:51,347 --> 00:22:55,675
by about six and a half degrees
for every kilometre on average.

341
00:22:56,025 --> 00:22:59,735
This lowest layer, where
the temperature falls with height,

342
00:22:59,735 --> 00:23:01,370
is called the troposphere,

343
00:23:01,370 --> 00:23:06,150
from a Greek word for turning, because
its air keeps overturning and mixing.

344
00:23:06,500 --> 00:23:11,600
Almost all our weather happens here:
the clouds, the rain and the storms.

345
00:23:12,400 --> 00:23:18,300
At about eleven kilometres, at about
minus sixty-three degrees, the fall stops.

346
00:23:18,650 --> 00:23:21,700
This boundary is called the tropopause.

347
00:23:22,050 --> 00:23:27,000
Above it, the temperature stops falling,
and higher up it rises again.

348
00:23:27,350 --> 00:23:30,897
That's the stratosphere,
a calm, layered region,

349
00:23:30,897 --> 00:23:35,775
where ozone absorbs the sun's
ultraviolet light and warms the air.

350
00:23:36,125 --> 00:23:39,528
Balloons usually burst
at around thirty kilometres,

351
00:23:39,528 --> 00:23:43,531
but other measurements show
the warming going on above that,

352
00:23:43,531 --> 00:23:46,200
up to about fifty kilometres on average.

353
00:23:46,550 --> 00:23:48,817
Above that come the mesosphere and

354
00:23:48,817 --> 00:23:52,150
the thermosphere, where
the air is extremely thin.

355
00:23:52,950 --> 00:23:57,050
Now let's keep the time the same
and change only the place.

356
00:23:57,400 --> 00:24:02,400
Here is a balloon launched at the same
moment at Singapore, near the equator.

357
00:24:02,750 --> 00:24:08,047
Its tropopause is much higher, at about
sixteen or seventeen kilometres,

358
00:24:08,047 --> 00:24:11,725
and much colder, below
minus seventy-five degrees.

359
00:24:12,075 --> 00:24:16,614
Strong rising currents in the warm
tropics stir the air much higher,

360
00:24:16,614 --> 00:24:18,950
so the troposphere is deeper there.

361
00:24:19,300 --> 00:24:23,700
Near the poles, it's shallower,
about eight to ten kilometres.

362
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:29,725
Why does the air get colder as we go
up, when that brings us closer to the sun?

363
00:24:30,075 --> 00:24:33,942
Because sunlight mostly passes
straight through the air and

364
00:24:33,942 --> 00:24:35,450
warms the ground first.

365
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:38,646
The ground then warms the air touching it,

366
00:24:38,646 --> 00:24:43,525
so the troposphere is heated from
below, like a pan of water on a stove.

367
00:24:43,875 --> 00:24:47,003
Here's a real sunny
morning over northern France,

368
00:24:47,003 --> 00:24:51,600
on the twenty-fourth of June, twenty
twenty-four, seen from a satellite.

369
00:24:51,950 --> 00:24:57,225
Heights in this picture are drawn twenty
times taller than distances on the map.

370
00:24:57,575 --> 00:25:01,900
Warm air near the ground
rises, in bubbles called thermals.

371
00:25:02,250 --> 00:25:06,625
As it rises, it moves into
lower pressure, so it expands.

372
00:25:06,975 --> 00:25:10,167
Expanding air pushes
its surroundings away,

373
00:25:10,167 --> 00:25:13,359
and that work costs it
energy, so it cools:

374
00:25:13,359 --> 00:25:19,150
by almost ten degrees for every kilometre
it rises, as long as no cloud forms.

375
00:25:19,500 --> 00:25:23,654
A weather balloon launched near
Paris at midday that day agrees:

376
00:25:23,654 --> 00:25:25,017
in the lowest one and

377
00:25:25,017 --> 00:25:30,275
a half kilometres, the temperature fell
by about ten degrees for every kilometre.

378
00:25:31,775 --> 00:25:36,452
By the early afternoon, the rising
air had cooled enough to make clouds,

379
00:25:36,452 --> 00:25:39,375
and the satellite saw
the sky fill with them.

380
00:25:39,725 --> 00:25:43,875
We'll work out exactly where
those clouds begin a little later.

381
00:25:44,675 --> 00:25:46,650
Now let's add water.

382
00:25:47,000 --> 00:25:51,100
Water vapour is water
as a gas, and it's invisible.

383
00:25:51,450 --> 00:25:56,300
Clouds and fog are not vapour:
they're tiny drops of liquid water.

384
00:25:56,650 --> 00:26:01,094
The amount of vapour is often
measured by the specific humidity:

385
00:26:01,094 --> 00:26:04,150
the grams of vapour
in each kilogram of air.

386
00:26:04,500 --> 00:26:07,700
It goes from almost zero in cold polar air

387
00:26:07,700 --> 00:26:10,976
to about twenty grams
per kilogram in warm,

388
00:26:10,976 --> 00:26:12,500
steamy tropical air.

389
00:26:12,850 --> 00:26:16,684
Vapour also adds its own small
share to the air pressure,

390
00:26:16,684 --> 00:26:18,500
called the vapour pressure.

391
00:26:19,300 --> 00:26:21,325
How big is that share?

392
00:26:21,675 --> 00:26:27,750
There's a handy rule for it: e is about
one point six one, times q, times p.

393
00:26:28,100 --> 00:26:30,227
Here e is the vapour pressure,

394
00:26:30,227 --> 00:26:35,757
q is the specific humidity written in
kilograms of vapour per kilogram of air,

395
00:26:35,757 --> 00:26:37,600
and p is the air pressure.

396
00:26:37,950 --> 00:26:42,254
Careful: this e is just the usual
letter for vapour pressure,

397
00:26:42,254 --> 00:26:44,300
not the number e from before.

398
00:26:44,650 --> 00:26:47,525
Where does one point six one come from?

399
00:26:47,875 --> 00:26:50,663
Pressure comes from molecules
hitting things,

400
00:26:50,663 --> 00:26:54,875
so the vapour's share of the pressure
is its share of the molecules.

401
00:26:55,225 --> 00:26:59,197
A water molecule is lighter
than an average air molecule:

402
00:26:59,197 --> 00:27:04,075
it weighs about eighteen atomic mass
units, against about twenty-nine.

403
00:27:04,425 --> 00:27:09,298
So a kilogram of vapour holds about
twenty-nine divided by eighteen,

404
00:27:09,298 --> 00:27:14,100
or one point six one, times as many
molecules as a kilogram of air.

405
00:27:14,450 --> 00:27:19,087
That's why the vapour's share
of the molecules, and so of the pressure,

406
00:27:19,087 --> 00:27:22,875
is about one point six one
times its share of the mass, q.

407
00:27:23,225 --> 00:27:28,362
Let's try the steamiest tropical air:
q is twenty grams per kilogram,

408
00:27:28,362 --> 00:27:33,500
which is zero point zero two, and p is
about a thousand hectopascals.

409
00:27:33,850 --> 00:27:39,435
So e is about one point six one times
zero point zero two times a thousand,

410
00:27:39,435 --> 00:27:44,200
which is about thirty-two hectopascals,
out of about a thousand.

411
00:27:45,700 --> 00:27:49,322
Over a water surface,
molecules leave the water and

412
00:27:49,322 --> 00:27:51,950
others land back in it, all the time.

413
00:27:52,300 --> 00:27:57,375
The fastest ones escape, and warmer
water has more fast molecules.

414
00:27:57,725 --> 00:28:01,380
When as many land as leave, the air
above is saturated,

415
00:28:01,380 --> 00:28:05,700
and its vapour pressure is called
the saturation vapour pressure.

416
00:28:06,050 --> 00:28:09,866
It depends only on temperature,
and it climbs steeply:

417
00:28:09,866 --> 00:28:12,623
about six hectopascals at zero degrees,

418
00:28:12,623 --> 00:28:17,075
twelve at ten, twenty-three
at twenty, and forty-two at thirty.

419
00:28:17,425 --> 00:28:21,150
It roughly doubles for every
ten degrees of warming.

420
00:28:21,500 --> 00:28:26,500
People often say warm air can hold
more water, like a bigger sponge.

421
00:28:26,850 --> 00:28:30,516
Unlike a sponge, though, the air
isn't doing the holding:

422
00:28:30,516 --> 00:28:35,725
the limit is set by the temperature, and
it would be the same with no air at all.

423
00:28:36,525 --> 00:28:39,350
Relative humidity compares the two.

424
00:28:39,700 --> 00:28:44,281
It's the vapour pressure divided
by the saturation vapour pressure,

425
00:28:44,281 --> 00:28:45,375
as a percentage.

426
00:28:45,725 --> 00:28:51,650
Let's try air at twenty-five degrees with
a vapour pressure of fifteen hectopascals.

427
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:53,578
At twenty-five degrees,

428
00:28:53,578 --> 00:28:57,900
the saturation vapour pressure
is about thirty-one point three.

429
00:28:58,250 --> 00:29:02,793
Fifteen divided by thirty-one point three
is about point four eight,

430
00:29:02,793 --> 00:29:06,400
so the relative humidity
is about forty-eight percent.

431
00:29:07,900 --> 00:29:12,375
Notice that it depends on the temperature
as well as on the water.

432
00:29:12,725 --> 00:29:15,365
Cool the same air to eighteen degrees,

433
00:29:15,365 --> 00:29:19,741
and its relative humidity rises
to about seventy-three percent,

434
00:29:19,741 --> 00:29:21,825
even though no water is added.

435
00:29:22,625 --> 00:29:26,625
So how far must we cool this
air before it's saturated?

436
00:29:26,975 --> 00:29:29,155
We need the temperature at which

437
00:29:29,155 --> 00:29:33,650
the saturation vapour pressure has
fallen to fifteen hectopascals.

438
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,375
That's about thirteen degrees.

439
00:29:37,875 --> 00:29:41,101
This is the dew point:
the temperature to which

440
00:29:41,101 --> 00:29:45,150
air must be cooled, keeping
its water, to become saturated.

441
00:29:45,500 --> 00:29:46,687
On a clear night,

442
00:29:46,687 --> 00:29:51,575
grass cools below the dew point of the air
touching it, and dew forms.

443
00:29:51,925 --> 00:29:57,000
When a whole layer of air near the ground
cools to its dew point, we get fog.

444
00:29:57,350 --> 00:30:01,575
And when rising air cools
to its dew point, we get a cloud.

445
00:30:02,375 --> 00:30:06,300
Let's follow that air parcel
as it rises from the ground.

446
00:30:06,650 --> 00:30:10,525
It cools by almost ten
degrees for every kilometre.

447
00:30:10,875 --> 00:30:15,944
Its dew point falls too, but slowly, by
about two degrees per kilometre,

448
00:30:15,944 --> 00:30:20,450
because as the parcel expands,
its vapour is spread more thinly.

449
00:30:20,800 --> 00:30:24,014
So the gap between
the temperature and the dew point,

450
00:30:24,014 --> 00:30:28,925
twelve degrees at the ground, shrinks by
about eight degrees for every kilometre.

451
00:30:29,275 --> 00:30:32,900
It closes after about one
and a half kilometres.

452
00:30:34,400 --> 00:30:38,075
There the vapour condenses,
and the cloud begins.

453
00:30:38,425 --> 00:30:41,480
That's why fair-weather
clouds have flat bases:

454
00:30:41,480 --> 00:30:46,875
parcels rising from the same ground reach
their dew point at about the same height.

455
00:30:47,675 --> 00:30:49,675
Now let's look at wind.

456
00:30:50,025 --> 00:30:53,253
Wind is air moving across
the Earth's surface,

457
00:30:53,253 --> 00:30:58,025
and it has both a speed and a direction,
so it's a vector: an arrow.

458
00:30:58,375 --> 00:31:02,325
We can split the arrow into
two parts, called components.

459
00:31:02,675 --> 00:31:07,350
The eastward part is written u, and
the northward part is written v.

460
00:31:07,700 --> 00:31:10,714
Take a wind blowing
towards the north-east,

461
00:31:10,714 --> 00:31:14,850
with u equal to eight and v equal
to six metres per second.

462
00:31:15,200 --> 00:31:20,531
By Pythagoras, its speed is the square
root of eight squared plus six squared,

463
00:31:20,531 --> 00:31:22,650
which is ten metres per second.

464
00:31:24,150 --> 00:31:29,100
Meteorologists name a wind by where
it comes from, not where it's going.

465
00:31:29,450 --> 00:31:33,500
A westerly wind blows from the west,
towards the east.

466
00:31:33,850 --> 00:31:36,174
The direction is given in degrees,

467
00:31:36,174 --> 00:31:39,523
measured clockwise from north
as seen from above:

468
00:31:39,523 --> 00:31:42,873
north is zero, east
ninety, south one hundred and

469
00:31:42,873 --> 00:31:45,675
eighty, and west two hundred and seventy.

470
00:31:46,025 --> 00:31:50,750
Our wind blows towards the north-east,
so it comes from the south-west.

471
00:31:51,100 --> 00:31:53,578
Its direction is about two hundred and

472
00:31:53,578 --> 00:31:57,100
thirty-three degrees,
and we call it a south-westerly.

473
00:31:58,600 --> 00:32:01,918
Careful: ocean currents
use the opposite habit,

474
00:32:01,918 --> 00:32:07,425
and are named by where they go, so an
eastward current flows towards the east.

475
00:32:08,225 --> 00:32:11,050
Wind speed comes in several units.

476
00:32:11,400 --> 00:32:14,950
Metres per second is the scientific one.

477
00:32:15,300 --> 00:32:19,473
Multiply by three point six
to get kilometres per hour,

478
00:32:19,473 --> 00:32:23,950
so ten metres per second
is thirty-six kilometres per hour.

479
00:32:24,300 --> 00:32:26,875
Sailors and pilots use knots.

480
00:32:27,225 --> 00:32:29,639
A knot is one nautical mile per hour,

481
00:32:29,639 --> 00:32:32,967
and a nautical mile, one
thousand eight hundred and

482
00:32:32,967 --> 00:32:34,077
fifty-two metres,

483
00:32:34,077 --> 00:32:38,775
is about one sixtieth of a degree
of latitude along the Earth's surface.

484
00:32:39,125 --> 00:32:42,864
So one knot is about point five one
metres per second,

485
00:32:42,864 --> 00:32:46,950
and our ten metres per second
wind is about nineteen knots.

486
00:32:48,450 --> 00:32:50,525
Why does air move at all?

487
00:32:50,875 --> 00:32:53,350
The simplest reason is pressure.

488
00:32:53,700 --> 00:32:57,258
Where the pressure is higher
on one side than on the other,

489
00:32:57,258 --> 00:33:00,757
the air is pushed from high
pressure towards low pressure,

490
00:33:00,757 --> 00:33:03,350
just as air rushes out
of a punctured tyre.

491
00:33:03,700 --> 00:33:07,911
But on our spinning Earth, moving
air is gradually turned aside,

492
00:33:07,911 --> 00:33:12,714
so over large distances the wind ends
up blowing around areas of high and

493
00:33:12,714 --> 00:33:15,675
low pressure, rather
than straight into them.

494
00:33:16,025 --> 00:33:21,142
We'll see how that turning works in later
videos on the Coriolis effect and

495
00:33:21,142 --> 00:33:22,575
the geostrophic wind.

496
00:33:23,375 --> 00:33:27,350
Before we look at real
maps, let's pin down two words.

497
00:33:27,700 --> 00:33:33,190
Weather is the state of the atmosphere
at one place and time: its temperature,

498
00:33:33,190 --> 00:33:38,750
pressure, humidity, wind, clouds and
rain, right now or over the next few days.

499
00:33:39,100 --> 00:33:43,940
Climate is the statistics of the
weather over many years, usually thirty:

500
00:33:43,940 --> 00:33:48,050
what's normal, and how far
the weather usually strays from it.

501
00:33:48,400 --> 00:33:51,045
Here is the daily temperature at Brest,

502
00:33:51,045 --> 00:33:55,250
in north-western France, for every
day of twenty twenty-three.

503
00:33:55,600 --> 00:33:59,085
Behind it is Brest's climate
for each day of the year,

504
00:33:59,085 --> 00:34:02,571
the average over nineteen
ninety-one to twenty twenty,

505
00:34:02,571 --> 00:34:05,475
with a band that holds
eight days out of ten.

506
00:34:05,825 --> 00:34:08,975
The weather line jumps
around from day to day,

507
00:34:08,975 --> 00:34:12,125
but most of the time it
stays inside the band.

508
00:34:12,475 --> 00:34:16,275
Climate is what you expect;
weather is what you get.

509
00:34:17,075 --> 00:34:19,550
How do we know the weather everywhere?

510
00:34:19,900 --> 00:34:23,371
From observations:
thousands of weather stations,

511
00:34:23,371 --> 00:34:27,975
weather balloons, ships, buoys
and aircraft, and many satellites.

512
00:34:28,325 --> 00:34:30,125
But they're uneven.

513
00:34:30,475 --> 00:34:35,075
Here are the places that launched
weather balloons in twenty twenty-three.

514
00:34:35,425 --> 00:34:39,465
They're crowded over Europe, North
America and East Asia,

515
00:34:39,465 --> 00:34:43,575
but sparse over the oceans, much
of Africa, and the poles.

516
00:34:43,925 --> 00:34:48,250
And each instrument measures
different things, at different times.

517
00:34:49,050 --> 00:34:51,700
A reanalysis fills in the gaps.

518
00:34:52,050 --> 00:34:54,806
It starts from a weather forecast model,

519
00:34:54,806 --> 00:34:59,353
a computer program that steps
the laws of physics forward in time,

520
00:34:59,353 --> 00:35:03,625
including the hydrostatic balance
and the gas law we just met.

521
00:35:03,975 --> 00:35:05,085
Every few hours,

522
00:35:05,085 --> 00:35:09,733
it nudges the model towards all
the observations made at that time,

523
00:35:09,733 --> 00:35:12,300
trusting the more reliable ones more.

524
00:35:12,650 --> 00:35:16,100
The result is a complete
picture of the atmosphere,

525
00:35:16,100 --> 00:35:19,550
at every place and height, every
hour, for decades.

526
00:35:19,900 --> 00:35:23,179
The one we use in this
video is called ERA5,

527
00:35:23,179 --> 00:35:27,875
made by the European Centre
for Medium-Range Weather Forecasts.

528
00:35:28,225 --> 00:35:30,865
It runs from nineteen forty to today,

529
00:35:30,865 --> 00:35:34,575
on a grid with points about
thirty kilometres apart.

530
00:35:34,925 --> 00:35:37,547
It's like a jigsaw with missing pieces,

531
00:35:37,547 --> 00:35:41,850
filled in using the pieces around
them and the rules of physics.

532
00:35:42,200 --> 00:35:43,947
Unlike a photograph, though,

533
00:35:43,947 --> 00:35:48,003
where observations are sparse
the result leans more on the model,

534
00:35:48,003 --> 00:35:49,750
so it's least certain there.

535
00:35:50,550 --> 00:35:52,925
Now let's make a pressure map.

536
00:35:53,275 --> 00:35:57,285
Here is the real air pressure
at the ground all over the world,

537
00:35:57,285 --> 00:36:02,250
at midday on the second of November,
twenty twenty-three, from the reanalysis.

538
00:36:02,600 --> 00:36:05,025
What stands out are the mountains.

539
00:36:05,375 --> 00:36:07,342
Over Tibet and the Himalayas,

540
00:36:07,342 --> 00:36:11,751
the pressure at the ground is less
than six hundred hectopascals,

541
00:36:11,751 --> 00:36:14,600
and the Andes and the Rockies show up too.

542
00:36:14,950 --> 00:36:20,025
In the close-up, the mountains are drawn
thirty times taller than they really are.

543
00:36:20,375 --> 00:36:25,175
That's just hydrostatic balance:
high ground has less air above it.

544
00:36:25,525 --> 00:36:29,525
The weather is in there, but
these mountain patterns swamp it.

545
00:36:30,325 --> 00:36:34,275
So how do we compare a mountain
town with a seaside town?

546
00:36:34,625 --> 00:36:39,025
We imagine what the pressure would
be if each place were at sea level.

547
00:36:39,375 --> 00:36:43,113
Suppose a weather station sits
one thousand six hundred and

548
00:36:43,113 --> 00:36:47,168
nine metres up, about the height
of Denver in the United States,

549
00:36:47,168 --> 00:36:50,526
and reads eight hundred
and thirty-five hectopascals,

550
00:36:50,526 --> 00:36:53,250
with an air temperature
of fifteen degrees.

551
00:36:53,600 --> 00:36:56,848
We add the weight
of an imaginary column of air,

552
00:36:56,848 --> 00:36:59,825
reaching from the station
down to sea level.

553
00:37:00,175 --> 00:37:04,654
With our exponential rule run
downwards, that's eight hundred and

554
00:37:04,654 --> 00:37:07,755
thirty-five times e to
the power of one point

555
00:37:07,755 --> 00:37:10,925
six oh nine divided by
eight point four three.

556
00:37:11,275 --> 00:37:14,868
That's about one thousand
and ten hectopascals:

557
00:37:14,868 --> 00:37:17,850
a perfectly ordinary sea-level reading.

558
00:37:19,350 --> 00:37:22,050
This is called the sea-level pressure.

559
00:37:22,400 --> 00:37:27,850
Real weather services use a slightly
more careful version of the same idea.

560
00:37:28,650 --> 00:37:32,700
Here is the same moment
again, now as sea-level pressure.

561
00:37:33,050 --> 00:37:36,375
The mountains are
gone, and the weather appears.

562
00:37:36,725 --> 00:37:40,868
Let's join the places that have
the same sea-level pressure,

563
00:37:40,868 --> 00:37:42,525
every four hectopascals.

564
00:37:42,875 --> 00:37:47,525
These lines are called isobars,
from Greek words for equal weight.

565
00:37:47,875 --> 00:37:49,968
Where the isobars crowd together,

566
00:37:49,968 --> 00:37:54,850
the pressure changes quickly over a short
distance, and the winds are strong.

567
00:37:55,650 --> 00:38:00,546
The map shows round patches where
the pressure is higher than all around,

568
00:38:00,546 --> 00:38:05,375
called highs, and patches where
it's lower than all around, called lows.

569
00:38:05,725 --> 00:38:09,365
A low is also called a depression,
or a cyclone,

570
00:38:09,365 --> 00:38:12,475
and a high is also called an anticyclone.

571
00:38:12,825 --> 00:38:18,238
Over the east coast of England, at the
edge of the North Sea, is a deep low,

572
00:38:18,238 --> 00:38:22,725
Storm Ciarán, at about nine
hundred and fifty-six hectopascals.

573
00:38:23,075 --> 00:38:26,456
Far to the south-west,
near the Azores islands,

574
00:38:26,456 --> 00:38:29,550
is a high at about one
thousand and thirty.

575
00:38:29,900 --> 00:38:31,988
Here is a much more recent map,

576
00:38:31,988 --> 00:38:36,163
a weather-service analysis
from the twenty-seventh of October,

577
00:38:36,163 --> 00:38:37,375
twenty twenty-six.

578
00:38:37,725 --> 00:38:41,467
Its deepest low is a typhoon
over the western Pacific,

579
00:38:41,467 --> 00:38:45,001
at about nine hundred
and thirty-five hectopascals,

580
00:38:45,001 --> 00:38:49,297
while a low over the North Atlantic,
at about nine hundred and

581
00:38:49,297 --> 00:38:52,000
fifty-five, is about as deep as Ciarán.

582
00:38:52,800 --> 00:38:57,175
Lows and highs bring different
weather, and now we can see why.

583
00:38:57,525 --> 00:39:01,750
Near the ground, air drifts
in towards the centre of a low.

584
00:39:02,100 --> 00:39:04,950
It can't pile up there, so it rises.

585
00:39:05,300 --> 00:39:11,575
Rising air expands and cools, it reaches
its dew point, and clouds and rain form.

586
00:39:11,925 --> 00:39:15,650
That's why lows bring
cloudy, wet and windy weather.

587
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:20,600
Around a high, air sinks from above
and spreads out near the ground.

588
00:39:20,950 --> 00:39:23,334
Sinking air is squeezed and warms,

589
00:39:23,334 --> 00:39:27,400
so it moves away from its dew
point, and clouds evaporate.

590
00:39:27,750 --> 00:39:31,550
That's why highs usually
bring settled, dry weather.

591
00:39:32,350 --> 00:39:35,534
Now let's add the real
winds near the ground,

592
00:39:35,534 --> 00:39:39,000
measured ten metres
up, from the same reanalysis.

593
00:39:39,350 --> 00:39:43,225
Near Ciarán, the air doesn't
blow straight into the low.

594
00:39:43,575 --> 00:39:47,450
It swirls around it,
anticlockwise as seen from above,

595
00:39:47,450 --> 00:39:51,325
crossing the isobars only
slightly towards the centre.

596
00:39:51,675 --> 00:39:56,575
Around the high, it turns the other
way, clockwise as seen from above.

597
00:39:56,925 --> 00:40:00,125
In the southern hemisphere,
both are reversed.

598
00:40:00,475 --> 00:40:06,001
Why the wind circles instead of rushing
in is the story of the Coriolis effect,

599
00:40:06,001 --> 00:40:07,750
and it has its own video.

600
00:40:08,550 --> 00:40:12,150
Now let's look at temperature
and moisture together.

601
00:40:12,500 --> 00:40:17,477
Air that sits for days over one region
takes on its character: cold and

602
00:40:17,477 --> 00:40:22,525
dry over snowy land in the far
north, warm and moist over tropical seas.

603
00:40:22,875 --> 00:40:28,098
A huge body of air like this, often
more than a thousand kilometres across,

604
00:40:28,098 --> 00:40:32,625
with fairly even temperature
and humidity, is called an air mass.

605
00:40:32,975 --> 00:40:37,578
Air masses are named after where
they come from: polar or tropical,

606
00:40:37,578 --> 00:40:41,975
and maritime, from over the sea, or
continental, from over land.

607
00:40:42,325 --> 00:40:45,761
On the first of November,
twenty twenty-three, warm,

608
00:40:45,761 --> 00:40:49,527
moist tropical air was flowing
north-east towards France,

609
00:40:49,527 --> 00:40:53,425
ahead of Ciarán, while cooler
polar air followed behind it.

610
00:40:54,225 --> 00:40:58,075
Where two air masses meet, they
don't mix right away.

611
00:40:58,425 --> 00:41:02,678
The change from one to the other
is squeezed into a narrow zone,

612
00:41:02,678 --> 00:41:06,400
often a hundred kilometres
wide or less, called a front.

613
00:41:06,750 --> 00:41:09,201
Crossing a front, the temperature and

614
00:41:09,201 --> 00:41:13,175
the humidity change quickly,
and the wind changes direction.

615
00:41:13,525 --> 00:41:17,275
A front is named after
the air that's advancing.

616
00:41:17,625 --> 00:41:21,300
If cold air is pushing forward,
it's a cold front.

617
00:41:21,650 --> 00:41:25,200
If warm air is advancing,
it's a warm front.

618
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:28,850
Let's look at a cold front from the side.

619
00:41:29,200 --> 00:41:32,881
Here is a real slice through
the air around Storm Ciarán,

620
00:41:32,881 --> 00:41:37,273
from the reanalysis, at six
in the evening on the first of November,

621
00:41:37,273 --> 00:41:40,567
running from west to east
across the Bay of Biscay,

622
00:41:40,567 --> 00:41:43,150
right through the cold front on the map.

623
00:41:43,500 --> 00:41:46,351
The colours show how much
warmer or colder the

624
00:41:46,351 --> 00:41:49,015
air is than the average
at the same height,

625
00:41:49,015 --> 00:41:52,300
and the moving dots follow
the wind inside the slice.

626
00:41:52,650 --> 00:41:55,833
Remember the gas law:
at the same pressure,

627
00:41:55,833 --> 00:41:58,275
cold air is denser than warm air.

628
00:41:58,625 --> 00:42:04,550
So the advancing cold air stays low and
pushes in under the warm air like a wedge.

629
00:42:04,900 --> 00:42:05,867
In this slice,

630
00:42:05,867 --> 00:42:10,151
it rises about one kilometre
for every fifty kilometres along,

631
00:42:10,151 --> 00:42:14,850
and as it pushes forward, it lifts
the warm air ahead of it quickly.

632
00:42:15,200 --> 00:42:20,900
It cools to its dew point, and tall clouds
and heavy showers form in a narrow band.

633
00:42:21,250 --> 00:42:26,564
After the front passes, the air is colder
and drier, the pressure starts to rise,

634
00:42:26,564 --> 00:42:27,811
and the wind turns,

635
00:42:27,811 --> 00:42:33,125
in the northern hemisphere usually from
the south-west to the west or north-west.

636
00:42:33,925 --> 00:42:38,329
Now let's move north, to a second
slice that runs through Brest,

637
00:42:38,329 --> 00:42:40,600
where the warm front is arriving.

638
00:42:40,950 --> 00:42:45,340
At a warm front, the warm air
is advancing, but it's lighter,

639
00:42:45,340 --> 00:42:48,650
so it can't push the cold
air away from below.

640
00:42:49,000 --> 00:42:52,947
Instead, it glides up over the cold
air: in this slice,

641
00:42:52,947 --> 00:42:57,325
also about one kilometre up
for every fifty kilometres along,

642
00:42:57,325 --> 00:43:01,200
and in many storms warm
fronts slope even more gently.

643
00:43:01,550 --> 00:43:03,644
As it slides upwards it cools,

644
00:43:03,644 --> 00:43:08,112
and a broad sheet of layered cloud
forms far ahead of the front,

645
00:43:08,112 --> 00:43:10,625
bringing long spells of steady rain.

646
00:43:10,975 --> 00:43:16,280
When the warm front passes, the air turns
milder and moister, and the wind turns,

647
00:43:16,280 --> 00:43:21,650
in the northern hemisphere usually from
the south-east to the south or south-west.

648
00:43:22,000 --> 00:43:22,900
In this slice,

649
00:43:22,900 --> 00:43:27,402
heights are drawn eighty times taller
than distances along the ground,

650
00:43:27,402 --> 00:43:30,425
so the slopes look much
steeper than in nature.

651
00:43:31,225 --> 00:43:36,775
Here are real fronts, around Ciarán at six
in the evening on the first of November.

652
00:43:37,125 --> 00:43:41,232
The colours show the temperature
about one and a half kilometres up,

653
00:43:41,232 --> 00:43:44,675
where the ground's daily
warming and cooling matter less.

654
00:43:45,025 --> 00:43:49,400
The warm tropical air reaches
up towards Brittany in a tongue.

655
00:43:49,750 --> 00:43:53,700
Along its leading edge, to
the east, is the warm front.

656
00:43:54,050 --> 00:43:59,250
Along its trailing edge, curving away
to the south-west, is the cold front.

657
00:43:59,600 --> 00:44:03,412
The arrows show the wind turning
as it crosses the fronts,

658
00:44:03,412 --> 00:44:05,450
most sharply at the warm front.

659
00:44:06,250 --> 00:44:07,303
On weather maps,

660
00:44:07,303 --> 00:44:12,175
fronts are drawn as lines with symbols
on the side they're moving towards.

661
00:44:12,525 --> 00:44:17,950
A cold front has blue triangles,
and a warm front has red half circles.

662
00:44:18,300 --> 00:44:22,150
You'll learn to read these maps
in detail in the next video.

663
00:44:22,950 --> 00:44:28,000
Now let's zoom out to the whole planet,
and ask why the weather moves at all.

664
00:44:28,350 --> 00:44:30,350
It starts with sunlight.

665
00:44:30,700 --> 00:44:35,258
Picture a beam of sunlight one
metre wide, at noon at an equinox,

666
00:44:35,258 --> 00:44:39,675
in March or September,
when the sun is overhead at the equator.

667
00:44:40,025 --> 00:44:43,800
At the equator, it lands
on one metre of ground.

668
00:44:44,150 --> 00:44:47,957
At sixty degrees north, the ground
is tilted to the beam,

669
00:44:47,957 --> 00:44:50,895
and the same beam
is spread over two metres,

670
00:44:50,895 --> 00:44:53,500
so each square metre gets half as much.

671
00:44:55,000 --> 00:44:59,397
So the tropics are heated much more
than the poles, and the atmosphere and

672
00:44:59,397 --> 00:45:02,725
the ocean carry heat
from the tropics towards the poles.

673
00:45:03,075 --> 00:45:07,525
Storms like Ciarán are one
of the ways the atmosphere does it.

674
00:45:08,325 --> 00:45:12,800
Averaged over many years,
sea-level pressure falls into belts.

675
00:45:13,150 --> 00:45:17,324
Around thirty degrees north
and south, air sinks from high up,

676
00:45:17,324 --> 00:45:21,700
and large highs sit over the oceans, like
the Azores high we saw.

677
00:45:22,050 --> 00:45:24,675
These are the subtropical highs.

678
00:45:25,025 --> 00:45:27,934
Near the equator is a belt of low pressure

679
00:45:27,934 --> 00:45:31,120
where the winds from both
hemispheres meet and

680
00:45:31,120 --> 00:45:33,475
rise, building tall thunderclouds.

681
00:45:33,825 --> 00:45:37,225
It's called the intertropical
convergence zone.

682
00:45:37,575 --> 00:45:43,600
How these belts are made is the subject of
a later video on the general circulation.

683
00:45:44,400 --> 00:45:47,575
The winds near the ground
follow these belts.

684
00:45:47,925 --> 00:45:53,096
Between the subtropical highs and the
equator, steady winds blow from the east,

685
00:45:53,096 --> 00:45:57,875
called the trade winds; sailing ships
relied on them to cross the oceans.

686
00:45:58,225 --> 00:46:01,675
North of the equator,
they come from the north-east.

687
00:46:02,025 --> 00:46:04,875
South of it, they come
from the south-east.

688
00:46:05,225 --> 00:46:09,305
Poleward of the subtropical
highs, in the middle latitudes,

689
00:46:09,305 --> 00:46:12,900
the winds come mostly
from the west: the westerlies.

690
00:46:13,250 --> 00:46:17,875
That's why storms like Ciarán
usually travel from west to east.

691
00:46:18,225 --> 00:46:22,508
Why the winds bend like this
is part of the Coriolis story,

692
00:46:22,508 --> 00:46:24,250
coming in a later video.

693
00:46:25,050 --> 00:46:30,675
Now let's put all of this together on one
real storm, and watch it from one town.

694
00:46:31,025 --> 00:46:34,157
On the first of November,
twenty twenty-three,

695
00:46:34,157 --> 00:46:38,175
Storm Ciarán was racing across
the Atlantic towards Europe.

696
00:46:38,525 --> 00:46:41,481
Here it is from a satellite
that afternoon,

697
00:46:41,481 --> 00:46:44,025
with coastlines and borders drawn in.

698
00:46:44,375 --> 00:46:48,850
And here is Brest, a port city
at the western tip of France.

699
00:46:49,200 --> 00:46:54,825
Let's watch the storm pass over
Brest, hour by hour, using the reanalysis.

700
00:46:55,175 --> 00:47:00,725
Times are in universal time, the clock
that meteorologists everywhere share.

701
00:47:01,525 --> 00:47:05,245
Here are the isobars every
four hectopascals from midday

702
00:47:05,245 --> 00:47:08,500
on the first of November
to midday on the second,

703
00:47:08,500 --> 00:47:13,350
with the warm front in red and the cold
front in blue until that evening.

704
00:47:13,700 --> 00:47:17,291
The low deepens as it
comes, from about nine hundred and

705
00:47:17,291 --> 00:47:19,407
seventy to about nine hundred and

706
00:47:19,407 --> 00:47:20,945
fifty-four hectopascals,

707
00:47:20,945 --> 00:47:26,075
and its centre crosses the English Channel
just north of Brest during the night.

708
00:47:26,875 --> 00:47:29,325
First, the pressure at Brest.

709
00:47:29,675 --> 00:47:33,630
At midday on the first, it
reads about nine hundred and

710
00:47:33,630 --> 00:47:35,500
ninety-seven hectopascals.

711
00:47:35,850 --> 00:47:40,043
Then it falls, faster
and faster, to about nine hundred and

712
00:47:40,043 --> 00:47:44,875
sixty-two at one o'clock in the morning,
as the low passes close by.

713
00:47:45,225 --> 00:47:50,375
That's thirty-five hectopascals
in thirteen hours, a very fast fall.

714
00:47:51,875 --> 00:47:56,900
Then it climbs again, by about
fourteen hectopascals in five hours.

715
00:47:57,700 --> 00:48:00,075
Now the temperature and the wind.

716
00:48:00,425 --> 00:48:01,846
At six in the evening,

717
00:48:01,846 --> 00:48:06,820
the wind blows from the south-south-east
at about thirteen metres per second,

718
00:48:06,820 --> 00:48:09,275
and the temperature is twelve degrees.

719
00:48:09,625 --> 00:48:13,504
By eight, the wind has turned
to come from the south-south-west,

720
00:48:13,504 --> 00:48:16,899
it has strengthened to about
eighteen metres per second,

721
00:48:16,899 --> 00:48:20,475
and the temperature has risen
to about fourteen and a half.

722
00:48:20,825 --> 00:48:23,200
That's the warm front passing.

723
00:48:23,550 --> 00:48:29,225
Brest is now in the warm air behind it,
which is called the storm's warm sector.

724
00:48:30,025 --> 00:48:33,000
A few hours later comes the cold front.

725
00:48:33,350 --> 00:48:36,511
By now the storm has broken
and bent its fronts so

726
00:48:36,511 --> 00:48:39,230
much that the map can't
trace them cleanly,

727
00:48:39,230 --> 00:48:42,075
but Brest's own measurements
show it clearly.

728
00:48:42,425 --> 00:48:45,150
Between midnight and three in the morning,

729
00:48:45,150 --> 00:48:48,460
the wind swings from the
south-west to the west and

730
00:48:48,460 --> 00:48:49,887
strengthens: by three,

731
00:48:49,887 --> 00:48:53,975
it's about twenty-three metres
per second, or forty-five knots.

732
00:48:55,475 --> 00:48:59,004
The temperature drops by about
two degrees, to ten and

733
00:48:59,004 --> 00:49:02,075
a half, and the pressure
starts its fast climb.

734
00:49:02,425 --> 00:49:07,700
Cooler polar air has arrived behind
the front, just as our side view said.

735
00:49:08,500 --> 00:49:12,110
Here is the storm the next
afternoon, seen from space,

736
00:49:12,110 --> 00:49:17,125
now over the North Sea, with its clouds
wrapped in a spiral around the low.

737
00:49:17,475 --> 00:49:22,575
In the next video, we'll learn to read
weather maps like this one in detail.

738
00:49:22,925 --> 00:49:25,939
And later videos will explain why the wind

739
00:49:25,939 --> 00:49:29,025
circles the low instead
of rushing into it.

740
00:49:29,825 --> 00:49:33,156
To sum up, the atmosphere
is a thin shell of air,

741
00:49:33,156 --> 00:49:38,050
and almost all our weather happens
in its lowest layer, the troposphere.

742
00:49:38,400 --> 00:49:42,495
Air pressure is the weight
of the air above each square metre,

743
00:49:42,495 --> 00:49:46,325
about one thousand and thirteen
hectopascals at sea level.

744
00:49:46,675 --> 00:49:49,793
By hydrostatic balance,
it falls with height,

745
00:49:49,793 --> 00:49:54,575
roughly exponentially, with a scale
height of about eight kilometres.

746
00:49:54,925 --> 00:49:59,347
The gas law, p equals
rho R T, ties pressure, density and

747
00:49:59,347 --> 00:50:03,925
temperature together, so warm
air is lighter than cold air.

748
00:50:04,275 --> 00:50:08,537
Relative humidity compares
the vapour with its saturation value,

749
00:50:08,537 --> 00:50:11,600
and cooling air
to its dew point makes clouds.

750
00:50:11,950 --> 00:50:14,617
Winds are named by where they come from,

751
00:50:14,617 --> 00:50:17,750
and are measured in metres
per second or knots.

752
00:50:18,100 --> 00:50:21,199
Sea-level pressure maps
show highs and lows,

753
00:50:21,199 --> 00:50:24,650
and fronts are the boundaries
between air masses.

754
00:50:25,000 --> 00:50:29,591
Weather is what happens now,
climate is its long-term statistics,

755
00:50:29,591 --> 00:50:33,475
and a reanalysis gives us both,
everywhere, every hour.

756
00:50:34,275 --> 00:50:40,150
This video is one of a series of related
tutorials, so watch out for the others.

757
00:50:40,500 --> 00:50:43,408
They're made to help
students of meteorology,

758
00:50:43,408 --> 00:50:45,993
atmospheric science and climate science,

759
00:50:45,993 --> 00:50:49,611
and anyone else who wants
to learn not just these ideas,

760
00:50:49,611 --> 00:50:51,550
but the maths behind them too.

761
00:50:51,900 --> 00:50:56,812
The notebook for these weather basics, and
the notebooks for the other concepts,

762
00:50:56,812 --> 00:50:59,575
are on the website that
goes with this video.
